'When I use a word,' Humpty Dumpty said, in a rather scornful tone,'
it means just what I choose it to mean, neither more nor less.'

In addition to expressing desire, "want" has been used to mean things
lacking -- "Your hair wants cutting".  "Need" has rather famously
taken on emotional connotations -- "I need you to do this for me right
now", when what is meant is "I'd be pleased if you did this for me
right now" or "I'll be angry if you don't do this for me right now."

If we wanted (pun intended) to get ridiculous about this, we could
start talking about Maslow's Heirarchy of Needs.  But we've wasted
enough time already.


